EXT. TRACK, MUNICIPAL STADIUM - PRE-DAWN

Empty bleachers. Floodlights at half power. Frost on the infield. Lane lines glow white.

KALINDA, thirty-two, a distance runner in a thin jacket and tights, stretches against the starting block, one earbud in, her phone strapped to her upper arm. A bruise-colored sky pushes against the east wall.

She checks her watch. Taps her phone.

KALINDA (V.O.)
(her own recorded voice, wry)
Day one-oh-seven. Hamstring's tight. Don't be a hero.

Kalinda grimaces, a small laugh.

KALINDA
Noted.

She jogs a slow lap. Her breath puffs in the cold. At the far curve, she stops, retightens a lace.

KALINDA (V.O.)
Day ninety. Hit the wall at three miles. Cried a little. Kept going.

She straightens. Shakes out her arms. Breaks into a steady pace, long strides, breath in quarter time.

KALINDA (V.O.)
Day sixty. Told Mom I might retire. She said retire from what? I said running. She said, you never started.

Kalinda snorts. Rounds the curve.

KALINDA (V.O.)
Day one. Four hundred meters. Walked half. Felt like an idiot.

She lengthens her stride. The sky pinks.

KALINDA (V.O.)
Whoever you are today, run like you did on day one. Like you had nothing to prove.

She nods once to the empty stands. Runs on, and the floodlights, one by one, click off as dawn takes over.
